From b2c0fe3f8ada149b84ce2e65d0a9fd4e288fd319 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Michaud <jmichaud@igalia.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 10:19:57 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 19/23] [mya] Walk a snapshot's JS heap, and account for every
 libpas allocation Need the bug URL (OOPS!).

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

Corpse::HeapWalk walks the JS heap of a VM in a snapshot, reading every type
through the target's debug info (the previous patch), so the layouts are the
target's whatever it was built for.

- Opening: from a roots object the target records (a polymorphic object with
  `JSC::VM* vm` and `bool atSafePoint`), or from a VM's address and
  JavaScriptCore's image for a target that records none, such as a web
  process. Results are exact only when the roots say the target was at mya's
  safe point; otherwise they are marked unverified.
- Cells: forEachLiveCell visits the cells MarkedSpace::forEachLiveCell would
  inside a HeapIterationScope, stopping every allocator the way the target
  would, with the liveness rules of a heap between collections; precise
  allocations too. cellClass names a JS cell's C++ class from its
  Structure's ClassInfo, checked against the ClassInfo's size and parent.
  Class hierarchies that name their subclass with an enumerator rather than
  a vtable (WebCore's CSSValue) are read as the subclass.
- libpas: libpasAllocations/libpasRecords run libpas's own enumerator over
  the snapshot (through the root the target keeps for libmalloc on Darwin; on
  Linux, where libpas makes none, through a root mya relocates into the
  target). On Darwin, libpasPages accounts for libpas's footprint byte by
  byte: objects, free payload, metadata and what libpas records nothing in.
- Reach: from the roots, global variables, block headers and every live
  cell, the walk follows each pointer whose pointee type is known into the
  allocation it lands in, and reports how much of libpas's heap it reaches
  and how much it reads as a type, why it did not follow what it did not,
  and, for each allocation it missed, what holds it (a reached allocation,
  static data, other memory, other misses, or nothing).
- heapDump writes the result as a GCDebugging heap snapshot (version 3),
  which Web Inspector reads: a node per live cell and per other allocation,
  reached or missed, an edge per reference.

The HeapWalk, TypeFieldHierarchies and HeapDump suites check the walk against
the in-process heap (HeapIterationScope, HeapSnapshotBuilder and libpas's
enumerator run in the target), in and out of process, after the target
exits, away from a safe point, and before and after a change; the footprint
accounting is checked against phys_footprint on Darwin. testLibJSCTools now
initializes JSC quietly (no concurrent GC or JIT, no warm-up blocks) so that
nothing but the mutator touches the heap a test measures.

Needs the libpas export, HashTraits, Region, Linux, images, footprint and
debug info patches earlier in this series.

